 "name": "Orange Is the New Green: Schoolyard Trees and Perceived Restoration",
 "description": "Faithful replication with the paper's own stimuli: Paddle & Gilliland (2016), \"Orange Is the New Green: Exploring the Restorative Capacity of Seasonal Foliage in Schoolyard Trees\", International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health 13(5): 497. Participants see twelve computer-rendered views of a planned schoolyard greening design in London, Ontario, in random order and for at least 45 s each, and rate each on Han's Short Version Revised Restoration Scale (SRRS): two emotional, two physiological, two cognitive and two behavioural items on a 1-9 scale, worded as printed in the paper's Figure 4. Scenes cross foliage (in-leaf green, in-leaf orange, leafless) x two perspectives x planting (deciduous only vs 3:1 deciduous to evergreen conifers); images are the paper's Figure 5 (CC BY 4.0). Scoring: reverse the two physiological items, average subscales and all items, compare foliage conditions with paired tests and scenes with vs without evergreens. Built for children (the paper's sample had mean age 12.2) but works for any age. Differences from the original study: (1) online, one person at a time, instead of a class viewing a projector together; (2) no two practice images and no researcher explanation of the four dimensions; (3) scene order random instead of one fixed order; (4) images cropped from the figure, about 415 x 275 px; (5) age and gender added.",
